= Knowledge — Technical =

== Propositional knowledge ==
The dominant analysis literature concerns propositional knowledge:

<pre>
S knows that p
</pre>

The traditional tripartite analysis proposes:

<pre>
K(S,p) ↔ p ∧ B(S,p) ∧ J(S,p)
</pre>

where <code>B</code> represents belief and <code>J</code> epistemic justification.

Gettier-style cases challenge the sufficiency of this conjunction by constructing cases in which justification and truth are connected through luck.

== Post-Gettier strategies ==
Major strategies include:

'''No-false-lemmas conditions''' — exclude knowledge inferred through a relevant false premise.

'''Defeasibility theories''' — knowledge requires the absence of true defeating information that would undermine justification.

'''Reliabilism''' — knowledge/justification depends on truth-conducive belief-forming processes.

'''Sensitivity''' — roughly, if p were false, the subject would not believe p.

'''Safety''' — roughly, in nearby possible situations where the subject forms the belief in the same way, the belief is not easily false.

'''Virtue/competence theories''' — true belief must manifest an epistemic competence or intellectual virtue.

'''Knowledge-first epistemology''' — treats knowledge as primitive rather than decomposable into belief plus independent conditions.

These families overlap and each faces counterexamples.

== Internalism and externalism ==
Internalism and externalism concern which factors can determine positive epistemic standing.

Access internalists require relevant justifying factors to be reflectively accessible. Externalists allow process reliability, environmental conditions, causal history, or modal relations to truth to contribute even when the subject cannot fully access them.

The distinction affects both [[Justification/Technical|justification]] and knowledge analysis.

== Knowledge-how ==
Whether knowing how to perform an action reduces to propositional knowledge remains disputed.

Intellectualist accounts analyze knowledge-how in propositional terms; anti-intellectualist accounts treat practical ability or competence as epistemically distinct.

== Knowledge and evidence ==
On some views, one's evidence consists of what one knows. On others, experiences, reasons, propositions, or mental states can be evidence without themselves being knowledge.

This dispute illustrates why [[Evidence/Technical|evidence]] and knowledge cannot be defined circularly without an explicit theory.

== Knowledge-first consequences ==
Knowledge-first theories use knowledge to explain:
* evidence;
* assertion norms;
* rational action;
* belief;
* mental states such as seeing that p.

The approach changes the architecture of epistemology: instead of asking only what ingredients compose knowledge, it asks what knowledge can explain.

